{{Main|2022 Illinois's 1st congressional district election}}
{{see also|Illinois's 1st congressional district}}
Before the [[2020 United States redistricting cycle|2020 redistricting cycle]], the 1st district was primarily based in the [[South Side, Chicago|South Side]] of [[Chicago]]. Under the new congressional map, although the 1st district is still based in Chicago, including portions of [[Bronzeville, Chicago|Bronzeville]], [[Hyde Park, Chicago|Hyde Park]], [[Greater Grand Crossing, Chicago|Grand Crossing]], [[Morgan Park, Chicago|Morgan Park]], and [[Roseland, Chicago|Roseland]], it now reaches down to the southwest and takes in a collection of exurban and rural areas in [[Cook County]], [[Will County]], and [[Kankakee County]]. The former section is heavily black and the latter is heavily white; as a result, the district as a whole is slightly over 50% black.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ilga.gov/CongressionalDistrictMaps/House/Congressional%20District%201.pdf |title=Congressional District 1 Map}}</ref><ref name = "declares">{{cite news |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/jonathan-jackson-eric-carlson-congress-bobby-rush/ |title=Democrat Jonathan Jackson declares victory in Illinois 1st Congressional District}}</ref><ref name = "densely">{{cite news |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/politics/elections/ct-1st-congressional-district-20220517-3hvbckdhfvcgnprv2jq4a7b3oi-story.html |title=In race to replace US Rep. Bobby Rush, candidates try to break through the clutter in densely crowded field}}</ref> The incumbent was Democrat [[Bobby Rush]], who was re-elected with 73.8% of the vote in 2020.<ref name="2020election">{{cite web|website=NBC News|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-elections/connecticut-house-results|title=Connecticut House Election Results 2020|date=March 31, 2021|access-date=October 16, 2021|archive-date=October 16, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211016042756/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-elections/connecticut-house-results|url-status=live}}</ref> On January 3, 2022, Rush announced that he would retire rather than seek a sixteenth term in office.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://chicago.suntimes.com/metro-state/2022/1/3/22865665/bobby-rush-retire|title=Rep. Bobby Rush to retire after 15 terms|website=Chicago Sun-Times|last1=Sweet|first1=Lynn|author-link=Lynn Sweet|date=January 3, 2022|access-date=January 10, 2022|archive-date=January 7, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220107175402/https://chicago.suntimes.com/metro-state/2022/1/3/22865665/bobby-rush-retire|url-status=live}}</ref>

