{{see also|Illinois's 6th congressional district}}

Due to redistricting, the borders of several districts in the Chicago area changed dramatically. [[Illinois's 3rd congressional district|3rd District]] Incumbent [[Marie Newman]] was drawn into the Latino-majority [[Illinois's 4th congressional district|4th District]]. As a result, Newman decided to switch to the redrawn 6th district and announced that she would be running against 6th district incumbent [[Sean Casten]].<ref name="Draw" /> The new district contains about 40% of Newman's old district and about 25% of Casten's.<ref name="SeekNew">{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/newsletters/illinois-playbook/2021/10/25/illinois-dems-seek-2nd-latino-congressional-district-494829|title=Illinois Dems seek 2nd Latino congressional district|website=Politico|last1=Kapos|first1=Shia|date=October 25, 2021|access-date=October 29, 2021|archive-date=October 29, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029194808/https://www.politico.com/newsletters/illinois-playbook/2021/10/25/illinois-dems-seek-2nd-latino-congressional-district-494829|url-status=live}}</ref> In the opening stages of the campaign both Casten and Newman declared an intent to run on their legislative records, and declared that they would not campaign negatively against each other.<ref name="FaceOff">{{cite web|url=https://abc7chicago.com/marie-newman-sean-casten-illinois-congressional-district-map/11183569/|title=Incumbent Dem. Reps. Casten, Newman to face off after Illinois congressional district map redrawn|website=ABC 7 Chicago|last1=Wall|first1=Craig|date=October 31, 2021|access-date=October 31, 2021|archive-date=October 30, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030184127/https://abc7chicago.com/marie-newman-sean-casten-illinois-congressional-district-map/11183569/|url-status=live}}</ref>

